WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.318 --> 00:00:07.769
So, we've reached the quadratic reciprocity law and machine learning.

00:00:07.769 --> 00:00:13.464
Let's define the Jacobi table,

00:00:13.464 --> 00:00:18.679
which will consist of Legendre symbols.

00:00:18.679 --> 00:00:22.275
Let's draw it.

00:00:22.275 --> 00:00:32.728
Here, yellow represents 1, and blue represents -1.

00:00:32.728 --> 00:00:38.453
X and Y are the numbers of the prime numbers.

00:00:38.453 --> 00:00:40.817
I took a hundred prime numbers,

00:00:40.817 --> 00:00:43.512
placed them here on the X-axis,

00:00:43.512 --> 00:00:47.121
and placed the hundred prime numbers here too.

00:00:47.121 --> 00:00:56.658
And in each little pixel, I drew yellow or blue based on

00:00:56.658 --> 00:01:02.268
whether Q (let's call this axis Q) is a quadratic residue modulo p.

00:01:02.268 --> 00:01:05.524
This is the X-axis.

00:01:05.524 --> 00:01:10.770
Rooster here is excited, wants to add something :D

00:01:10.770 --> 00:01:14.249
We look and don't see any regularity.

00:01:14.249 --> 00:01:18.300
Interesting maze generator appears here (or QR codes).

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:25.322
Alright. Let's generate a train set for machine learning.

00:01:25.322 --> 00:01:29.189
We record a random p,

00:01:29.189 --> 00:01:31.477
a random q

00:01:31.477 --> 00:01:38.161
and the value of the function we want to learn to predict from p and q.

00:01:38.161 --> 00:01:42.029
This is my dataset.

00:01:42.029 --> 00:01:45.746
Next, I start generating features.

00:01:45.746 --> 00:01:48.567
Some factors.

00:01:48.567 --> 00:01:53.171
I understand that the Jacobi function is a function of p and q.

00:01:53.171 --> 00:02:00.000
I need to generate factors – properties of objects p and q.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:01.453
What properties do I know?

00:02:01.453 --> 00:02:06.115
For example, I know residues modulo 3, 5...

00:02:06.115 --> 00:02:14.205
Also, I can take (p-1)/2, because earlier I saw that something can depend on this number.

00:02:14.205 --> 00:02:19.047
And also, whether this number is divisible by 3 or not.

00:02:19.047 --> 00:02:24.609
I ended up with 4 here and another 4 – 8 factors.

00:02:24.609 --> 00:02:29.498
And t – this is what I want to predict. I took t from the data.

00:02:29.498 --> 00:02:33.521
The expression is complex, but read it like this:

00:02:33.521 --> 00:02:36.853
we run through the elements of the data list.

00:02:36.853 --> 00:02:38.821
Each one is a triple.

00:02:38.821 --> 00:02:42.388
And as we know, each element is a triplet.

00:02:42.388 --> 00:02:47.168
We save this triplet in the variables p, q, and t.

00:02:47.168 --> 00:02:54.886
t is precisely our target function.

00:02:54.886 --> 00:03:01.703
Let's see what the mean squared deviation from the target is.

00:03:01.703 --> 00:03:05.376
0.999...

00:03:05.376 --> 00:03:08.357
This means that the mean is zero,

00:03:08.357 --> 00:03:16.075
and further, -1 or 1 appear in the dataset with a probability of about 50%.

00:03:16.075 --> 00:03:20.612
We need to learn to predict something close to t,

00:03:20.612 --> 00:03:28.346
so that the standard deviation for the forecast deviation and target,

00:03:28.346 --> 00:03:30.718
is less than 1.

00:03:30.718 --> 00:03:33.714
That's our goal.

00:03:33.714 --> 00:03:35.519
Lets run it...

00:03:35.519 --> 00:03:40.150
In Mathematica there's a Predict function,

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:47.409
to which you need to pass an array of factors or FeatureSet.

00:03:47.409 --> 00:03:52.253
Then draw an arrow and then the target list.

00:03:52.253 --> 00:03:54.857
The first set of features from this list

00:03:54.857 --> 00:03:59.594
corresponds to the first element from the this list.

00:03:59.594 --> 00:04:05.183
It calculated what the StandartDeviation is.

00:04:05.183 --> 00:04:10.649
You can see from this graph that it couldn't reduce it.

00:04:10.649 --> 00:04:16.482
The standard deviation remained 1.

00:04:16.482 --> 00:04:18.643
So, failure.

00:04:18.643 --> 00:04:25.939
We couldn't use machine learning to guess any dependency

00:04:25.939 --> 00:04:28.270
and I immediately draw a conclusion:

00:04:28.270 --> 00:04:37.573
most likely, information about what the Legendre symbol is not encoded in these factors.

00:04:37.573 --> 00:04:41.100
Alright, failure is also a result.

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:46.152
Next, we do a very natural action from my point of view.

00:04:46.152 --> 00:04:49.254
We got an asymmetrical picture,

00:04:49.254 --> 00:04:53.438
but let's take this picture,

00:04:53.438 --> 00:04:55.748
reflect it about the yellow line,

00:04:55.748 --> 00:04:58.015
and multiply it by itself.

00:04:58.015 --> 00:05:01.031
Make the picture symmetrical.

00:05:04.438 --> 00:05:06.330
Defining...

00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:07.703
Drawing...

00:05:07.703 --> 00:05:17.061
Now, in cell (i, j), I have the product of two Legendre symbols.

00:05:17.061 --> 00:05:21.227
I don't know why I wrote it so complicated here...

00:05:21.227 --> 00:05:24.623
We could make it simpler.

00:05:24.623 --> 00:05:27.572
And now I see regularity.

00:05:27.572 --> 00:05:30.120
This is not what was expected from a symmetrical picture.

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:32.749
Symmetrical pictures can be more complex.

00:05:32.749 --> 00:05:34.898
Here it's much simpler.

00:05:34.898 --> 00:05:39.784
It's evident that if a prime number is yellow,

00:05:39.784 --> 00:05:44.062
then all the answers for it are yellow.

00:05:44.062 --> 00:05:46.420
Yellow color seems to win.

00:05:46.420 --> 00:05:51.434
As if yellow is 0, and blue is 1.

00:05:51.434 --> 00:05:54.383
And in the cell is drawn the product

00:05:54.383 --> 00:05:56.010
of the color of this prime number

00:05:56.010 --> 00:05:59.704
and the color of that prime number.

00:05:59.704 --> 00:06:01.971
Yellow color prevails.

00:06:01.971 --> 00:06:04.894
Curious, can we...

00:06:04.894 --> 00:06:07.396
Alright... here's a bigger picture.

00:06:07.396 --> 00:06:18.769
Curious, can we this time achieve something less than 1 through machine learning?

00:06:18.769 --> 00:06:20.863
Or rather, not 1.

00:06:20.863 --> 00:06:27.632
This time, the standard deviation will already be &lt; 1 even without any prediction.

00:06:27.632 --> 00:06:33.732
The standard deviation of the target – that's its mean deviation from the mean,

00:06:33.732 --> 00:06:39.383
and the mean is no longer 0, we have more yellow color.

00:06:40.767 --> 00:06:46.833
So, our goal is to get a value during machine learning that's less than 0.75

00:06:46.833 --> 00:06:48.833
Let's check...

00:06:56.008 --> 00:06:57.731
Took a while to think...

00:06:57.731 --> 00:06:58.231
Yes!

00:06:58.231 --> 00:07:04.075
A miracle happened. 0.0339

00:07:04.075 --> 00:07:11.676
We managed to make the standard deviation smaller than the original standard deviation.

00:07:11.676 --> 00:07:17.648
That means we were able to extract some useful information from those factors.

00:07:17.648 --> 00:07:22.513
Sometimes machine learning cannot predict the target exactly.

00:07:22.513 --> 00:07:25.972
Like here, the standard deviation is not 0.

00:07:25.972 --> 00:07:28.346
But the signals are important,

00:07:28.346 --> 00:07:32.402
that among these numbers there are some numbers, maybe all of them,

00:07:32.402 --> 00:07:37.821
from which the product of two Legendre symbols pq and qp depends.

00:07:40.887 --> 00:07:42.644
Moving on...

00:07:42.644 --> 00:07:47.763
Next, I would like to show that we could work not with DecisionTree,

00:07:47.763 --> 00:07:50.563
as it was automatically selected here,

00:07:50.563 --> 00:07:52.298
but with neural networks.

00:07:52.298 --> 00:07:54.778
For that, there's the NetTrain function.

00:07:54.778 --> 00:07:58.237
Here, the architecture of the neural network is described.

00:07:58.237 --> 00:08:00.249
First layer of size 5.

00:08:00.249 --> 00:08:03.240
Then this activation function.

00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:09.872
Ramp – it's the well-known Rectified Linear Unit (ReLU) function.

00:08:09.872 --> 00:08:12.544
Here it's called Ramp.

00:08:12.544 --> 00:08:14.066
Then a layer of size 4,

00:08:14.066 --> 00:08:17.036
then activation again.

00:08:17.036 --> 00:08:21.071
And finally, a neuron.

00:08:21.071 --> 00:08:28.587
In the end, we have one  neuron where the answer should be.

00:08:28.587 --> 00:08:31.473
Let's see how the training looks.

00:08:31.473 --> 00:08:37.189
In the NetTrain function, you can explicitly specify the LossFunction and also ValidationSet.

00:08:37.189 --> 00:08:43.076
Above, I divided the DataSet into two parts.

00:08:43.076 --> 00:08:46.898
The first 80 thousand is the TrainSet,

00:08:46.898 --> 00:08:52.700
and the next 20 thousand is the ValidationSet.

00:08:52.700 --> 00:08:57.309
And accordingly, fs and target for them.

00:09:00.310 --> 00:09:07.995
Here we have the loss on the Validation Set, already 10^-4.

00:09:07.995 --> 00:09:12.051
This is the mean squared deviation.

00:09:12.051 --> 00:09:16.844
So, the Standard Deviation, the square root of this number, is about 10^-2.

00:09:16.844 --> 00:09:21.496
Here he writes that he used the default here.

00:09:21.496 --> 00:09:24.189
So, success!

00:09:24.189 --> 00:09:27.925
Among the factors we made up,

00:09:27.925 --> 00:09:31.789
there are some factors that affect the target.

00:09:31.789 --> 00:09:35.845
Through trial and error, I find out that there are two factors.

00:09:35.845 --> 00:09:37.632
These ones.

00:09:37.632 --> 00:09:45.542
Well, and then it starts to be a bit of guessing and trying.

00:09:45.542 --> 00:09:54.090
And it turns out that this thing is always equal to 1.

00:09:54.090 --> 00:10:05.995
The product of two Legendre symbols multiplied by (-1)^((p-1)/2)((q-1)/2) always equals 1.

00:10:05.995 --> 00:10:08.496
And here's where you can grasp the concept of the yellow color.

00:10:08.496 --> 00:10:14.234
If you have a prime p such that (p - 1)/2 is divisible by 2,

00:10:14.234 --> 00:10:19.322
then inevitably, this multiplier becomes 1.

00:10:24.368 --> 00:10:30.305
You won't get -1 here, regardless of q.

00:10:30.305 --> 00:10:35.684
This explains the dominance of the yellow color.

00:10:35.684 --> 00:10:41.677
This theorem, that this product always equals 1,

00:10:41.677 --> 00:10:44.264
meaning always yellow.

00:10:44.264 --> 00:10:48.632
Carl Friedrich Gauss proved it in eight different ways.

00:10:48.632 --> 00:10:50.942
He considered it a very important theorem.

00:10:50.942 --> 00:10:52.954
And it truly is a very important theorem.

00:10:52.954 --> 00:10:55.818
It has many fascinating generalizations.

00:10:55.818 --> 00:11:05.108
Below, I'll leave a link to a video by Alexey Savvateev about quadratic reciprocity laws.

00:11:05.108 --> 00:11:07.993
That's how it's described on Wikipedia.

00:11:07.993 --> 00:11:11.793
We define the Legendre symbol as follows.

00:11:11.793 --> 00:11:15.678
Is there a solution to n^2 = q or not?

00:11:15.678 --> 00:11:20.628
And the law of quadratic reciprocity.

00:11:20.628 --> 00:11:23.036
Alright.

00:11:23.036 --> 00:11:28.561
The Jacobi symbol, it can be extended as follows.

00:11:28.561 --> 00:11:34.150
When you have the second argument here as the product of two numbers,

00:11:34.150 --> 00:11:38.131
it's then expanded into the product of two Jacobi symbols.

00:11:38.131 --> 00:11:40.419
And when the first place is the product of two numbers,

00:11:40.419 --> 00:11:44.676
it's also expanded into the product of two Jacobi symbols.

00:11:44.676 --> 00:11:50.594
So, the Jacobi symbol is a multiplicative function of two arguments.

00:11:50.594 --> 00:11:58.120
To find out the value of the Jacobi symbol, you need to expand it over all products,

00:11:58.120 --> 00:12:04.039
based on the factorization of the first argument and the factorization of the second argument.

00:12:04.039 --> 00:12:10.629
Then you get exclusively prime numbers as arguments for the Jacobi function

00:12:10.629 --> 00:12:14.578
and then you expand them as Legendre symbols.

00:12:19.496 --> 00:12:27.914
To be continued...

